###############################################################################
# SNRM — REST API smoke tests
#
# Format: VS Code "REST Client" (humao.rest-client). Click "Send Request" above
# each block, top to bottom — later requests reuse ids returned by earlier ones.
#
# INTELLIJ USERS: IntelliJ's HTTP Client cannot read another request's response
# with the {{name.response.body.$.field}} syntax used below. Replace each file
# variable with a response handler on the request that produced it, e.g. after
# the login request add:
#
#     > {% client.global.set("token", response.body.token); %}
#
# and delete the "@token = ..." line. The requests themselves need no changes.
#
# BEFORE YOU START
#   1. MySQL 8 running on localhost:3306 with the schema from db/bootstrap.sql,
#      and SNRM_DB_USER / SNRM_DB_PASSWORD set.
#   2. mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
#   3. Set @password below. If SNRM_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH is not configured, the
#      application prints a generated password to the log at startup — look for
#      "using a generated password for the research user". For a stable one:
#        mvnw.cmd test -Dtest=BcryptHashToolTest -Dsnrm.password=your-password -DfailIfNoTests=false
#
# UNITS ON THE WIRE (FR-13)
#   Every duration and every rate is a {value, unit} object. The two differ by
#   one field name, and it is not an accident — it follows the database
#   column names:
#
#     duration  {"value": 36,  "unit":     "HOUR"}   lead time, processing time
#     rate      {"value": 120, "timeUnit": "WEEK"}   capacity, demand, holding cost
#
#   spring.jackson.deserialization.fail-on-unknown-properties=true, so sending
#   "unit" inside a rate is a 400, not a silently ignored field. Request 33
#   proves it.
#
#   Omitting the whole object is allowed and means the field's empty form —
#   unconstrained for a capacity, zero for a duration, a demand or a holding
#   cost — stated in the network's period unit.
#
# EVENT TIMING IS UNIT-BEARING TOO
#   A disruption event's startOffset and duration are durations, not period
#   indexes: {"startOffset": {"value": 10, "unit": "DAY"}}, measured from the
#   start of the horizon. A scenario is project-scoped so it can be replayed
#   against every configuration variant, and those variants need not
#   share a period length — "period 4" would be a different moment in each.
#   Requests 25a onward exercise this.
#
# Requests 1-28 are the happy path and must be run in order, including the
# scenario block 25a-25p. Requests 25q-25ab and 29-42 are deliberate failures
# and depend on the happy path having run. Requests 44-56 cover the CSV/XLSX/XML
# import and export and need only a project, so they can be
# run straight after request 2. Two requests are documented but cannot be run
# yet: 37, which needs a simulation run to freeze a network, and 25u, which
# needs a node id from request 27 pasted in by hand.
###############################################################################

@host = http://localhost:8080
@baseUrl = {{host}}/api/v1
@username = researcher
@password = CHANGE_ME


### 0. Health — the only endpoint that needs no token. Expect 200 {"status":"UP"}.
GET {{host}}/actuator/health


###############################################################################
# HAPPY PATH
#
# Builds the four-node network of docs/time-units-worked-example.md: lead times
# of 6 h, 36 h and 2 weeks and dwell times of 12 h and 4 h, all on a 1-day
# period. That document works the conversion arithmetic through by hand and
# states the exact findings requests 20-24 must return.
###############################################################################

### 1. Log in. Expect 200 with a JWT.
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{username}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@token = {{login.response.body.$.token}}


### 2. Create a project. Expect 201 + Location: /api/v1/projects/{id}.
# The name carries a timestamp so the file can be re-run without hitting
# DUPLICATE_NAME on uq_project.
# @name createProject
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Automotive tier-1 case {{$timestamp}}"
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@projectId = {{createProject.response.body.$.id}}


### 3. Create the baseline network. Expect 201, version 1, editable true, and
# the default clock of V3__time_units.sql: periodLength 1 DAY, horizonPeriods
# 52, roundingPolicy NEAREST.
# @name createNetwork
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/networks
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Baseline",
  "baseline": true
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@networkId = {{createNetwork.response.body.$.id}}


### 4. Set the time base explicitly. Expect 200 with
# {"network": {...}, "validation": {...}} — the validation half is empty for now
# because the network has no durations yet, and suggestedPeriod is absent for
# the same reason: nothing constrains the choice.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "horizonPeriods": 52,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 5. Add node 1 of 4 — a supplier. posX/posY are the canvas drop point
# Capacity is a rate: 500 a day. processingTime is omitted, so it
# takes its empty form — zero, in the network's period unit.
# @name createSupplier
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "SUP-1",
  "type": "SUPPLIER",
  "capacity": { "value": 500, "timeUnit": "DAY" },
  "fixedCost": 1000,
  "varCost": 2.5,
  "failureProb": 0.02,
  "region": "EU-West",
  "lat": 48.8566,
  "lng": 2.3522,
  "posX": 100,
  "posY": 100
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@supplierId = {{createSupplier.response.body.$.id}}


### 6. Add node 2 of 4 — a plant. Capacity stated over a week rather than the
# period: 400 a week is 57.142857 a period, and the response still says 400 per
# WEEK because the API never restates what the user entered.
# The 12-hour changeover is half a period and will round to a whole one.
# @name createPlant
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "PLANT-1",
  "type": "PLANT",
  "capacity": { "value": 400, "timeUnit": "WEEK" },
  "processingTime": { "value": 12, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "fixedCost": 5000,
  "varCost": 8,
  "failureProb": 0.03,
  "region": "EU-West",
  "posX": 300,
  "posY": 100
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@plantId = {{createPlant.response.body.$.id}}


### 7. Add node 3 of 4 — a distribution centre. Its 4-hour dwell is a sixth of
# a period and will disappear entirely under NEAREST.
# @name createDc
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "DC-1",
  "type": "DC",
  "capacity": { "value": 250, "timeUnit": "DAY" },
  "processingTime": { "value": 4, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "fixedCost": 2000,
  "varCost": 1.2,
  "failureProb": 0.01,
  "region": "EU-North",
  "posX": 500,
  "posY": 100
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@dcId = {{createDc.response.body.$.id}}


### 8. Add node 4 of 4 — a customer. Only required fields; the rest take the
# column defaults. capacity is omitted, so it stays unconstrained — and null
# fields are dropped from responses (spring.jackson.default-property-inclusion=
# non_null), so expect a "capacity" object carrying only its timeUnit.
# @name createCustomer
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "CUST-1",
  "type": "CUSTOMER",
  "region": "EU-North",
  "posX": 700,
  "posY": 100
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@customerId = {{createCustomer.response.body.$.id}}


### 9. Add link 1 of 3 — SUP-1 to PLANT-1, a 6-hour truck leg. Expect 201 and
# the lead time echoed back as 6 HOUR, not as a period count.
# @name createLink1
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceNodeId": {{supplierId}},
  "targetNodeId": {{plantId}},
  "leadTime": { "value": 6, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "capacity": { "value": 300, "timeUnit": "DAY" },
  "unitCost": 1.5,
  "failureProb": 0.01
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@link1Id = {{createLink1.response.body.$.id}}


### 10. Add link 2 of 3 — PLANT-1 to DC-1, a day and a half.
# @name createLink2
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceNodeId": {{plantId}},
  "targetNodeId": {{dcId}},
  "leadTime": { "value": 36, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "capacity": { "value": 250, "timeUnit": "DAY" },
  "unitCost": 0.9
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@link2Id = {{createLink2.response.body.$.id}}


### 11. Add link 3 of 3 — DC-1 to CUST-1, an ocean leg of two weeks. This is
# the one duration in the network that divides evenly into a 1-day period.
# @name createLink3
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceNodeId": {{dcId}},
  "targetNodeId": {{customerId}},
  "leadTime": { "value": 2, "unit": "WEEK" },
  "unitCost": 3.2,
  "failureProb": 0.02
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@link3Id = {{createLink3.response.body.$.id}}


### 12. Bulk-patch node positions — one drag of a four-node selection.
# Expect 200 {"updated": 4, "nodes": [...]} with the new coordinates.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes/positions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "positions": [
    { "nodeId": {{supplierId}}, "posX": 120, "posY": 240 },
    { "nodeId": {{plantId}},    "posX": 320, "posY": 240 },
    { "nodeId": {{dcId}},       "posX": 520, "posY": 240 },
    { "nodeId": {{customerId}}, "posX": 720, "posY": 240 }
  ]
}


### 13. Bulk-patch node attributes — the property panel flushing.
# Omitted fields are left alone: SUP-1 keeps its region, costs and coordinates,
# and CUST-1's capacity stays unconstrained. A rate is patched whole — there is
# no way to change a unit without restating the value it applies to.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "nodes": [
    { "nodeId": {{supplierId}}, "capacity": { "value": 750, "timeUnit": "DAY" }, "failureProb": 0.05 },
    { "nodeId": {{customerId}}, "name": "CUST-1 (Nordics)" }
  ]
}


### 14. Bulk-patch link attributes. Note what request 20 then reports: raising
# link 1 from 6 to 8 hours does not save it — a third of a period still rounds
# to zero.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "links": [
    { "linkId": {{link1Id}}, "leadTime": { "value": 8, "unit": "HOUR" } },
    { "linkId": {{link2Id}}, "unitCost": 1.1, "failureProb": 0.02 }
  ]
}


### 15. Put link 1 back to the 6 hours the worked example uses, with a PUT this
# time — a full replacement, so the omitted capacity is cleared back to
# unconstrained.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/links/{{link1Id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "leadTime": { "value": 6, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "unitCost": 1.5,
  "failureProb": 0.01
}


### 16. Add a product to the project (project-scoped).
# @name createProduct
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/products
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Gearbox",
  "unitValue": 250
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@productId = {{createProduct.response.body.$.id}}


### 17. Give the customer demand for that product (upsert on the natural key).
# 120 a week on a 1-day period is 17.142857 a period to the engine, and 120 per
# WEEK for ever after to the API. initialInventory and safetyStock carry no
# unit: a stock level is a quantity at an instant, not a flow.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/nodes/{{customerId}}/products/{{productId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "demand": { "value": 120, "timeUnit": "WEEK" },
  "initialInventory": 0,
  "safetyStock": 0,
  "holdingCost": { "value": 0.4, "timeUnit": "DAY" }
}


### 18. Give the DC some opening stock, to show a node-product row with no
# demand. Both rates are omitted, so both become zero over the network's period
# unit — DAY.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/nodes/{{dcId}}/products/{{productId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "initialInventory": 400,
  "safetyStock": 120
}


### 19. Read the network back. Expect the clock alongside the usual fields:
# periodLength {value 1, unit DAY}, horizonPeriods 52, roundingPolicy NEAREST.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# TIME RESOLUTION — one request per resolution check
###############################################################################

### 20. The editor's view of the current clock. Expect 200,
# errorCount 0, warningCount 4, suggestedPeriod {value 2, unit HOUR}, and these
# four findings in this order — worst first, then node before link, then by id:
#
#   NODE PLANT-1  processingTime  DURATION_ROUNDING_ERROR   12 h  -> 1 period   +100%
#   NODE DC-1     processingTime  DURATION_ROUNDS_TO_ZERO    4 h  -> 0 periods  -100%
#   LINK SUP-1 -> PLANT-1  leadTime  DURATION_ROUNDS_TO_ZERO 6 h  -> 0 periods  -100%
#   LINK PLANT-1 -> DC-1   leadTime  DURATION_ROUNDING_ERROR 36 h -> 2 periods   +33%
#
# The 2-week leg produces nothing: 14 days is 14 periods exactly.
# docs/time-units-worked-example.md works all of this through by hand.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-validation
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 21. The same network as the import wizard sees it. Only
# the severities change: the two DURATION_ROUNDS_TO_ZERO findings become errors,
# so errorCount 2, warningCount 2, and they sort to the top.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-validation?context=IMPORT
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 22. Accept the suggestion: 2 hours is the coarsest period that keeps every
# duration in this network exact. Expect 200 and an empty findings array —
# 6 h = 3 periods, 36 h = 18, 2 weeks = 168, 12 h = 6, 4 h = 2. The horizon is
# raised to keep the run covering the same span of real time; changing the
# period alone would have silently shortened it from 52 days to 4 days and 8
# hours.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 2, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "horizonPeriods": 624,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 23. PERIOD_TOO_FINE — the period is more than 1000x finer than the
# longest declared duration. At a 1-minute period the 2-week leg needs 20,160
# steps, and each of the 100 default replications pays for all of them.
# Expect exactly one finding: PERIOD_TOO_FINE, WARNING, on link 3. Every other
# duration still converts exactly, so nothing else is reported.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "MINUTE" },
  "horizonPeriods": 74880,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 24. Back to the 1-day period the worked example uses, so the rest of the
# file runs against the documented network.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "horizonPeriods": 52,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 25. EVENT_EXCEEDS_HORIZON — now runnable.
#
# It needs a scenario with an event, and the scenario endpoints exist
# as of requests 25a onward. Sent now, with no scenario named, this returns the
# same four findings as request 20: without a scenarioId there are no event
# windows to check, and that is the point of the parameter. Scenarios are
# project-scoped so they can be replayed against every variant —
# whether an event outruns the horizon is a question about the *pair*, so the
# request has to name both.
#
# Requests 25ac–25ae build the case that actually produces the finding.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-validation
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# DISRUPTION SCENARIOS (FR-05)
#
# Two levels, and the split is the whole design. A SCENARIO belongs to the
# PROJECT — one disruption story replayed against every configuration variant,
# which is what makes their results comparable. An EVENT belongs to the scenario
# but is authored against a NETWORK: its target is a node id, a link id or a
# region tag, and its window has to fit inside a horizon. Neither can be checked
# without naming a network, so every event write carries ?networkId= — and the
# server does not store it.
#
# TIMING IS UNIT-BEARING (FR-13). startOffset and duration are real
# durations measured from the start of the horizon:
#
#   {"startOffset": {"value": 10, "unit": "DAY"},
#    "duration":    {"value": 2,  "unit": "WEEK"}}
#
# NOT a start period and a period count. "Period 4" would be a different moment
# in a variant stepping in hours than in one stepping in weeks, and the
# comparison the scenario exists to support would be measuring the units. The
# timeline positions its bars by the converted value and labels them
# with the declared one.
#
# On the 1-day / 52-period clock restored by request 24, the events below land
# at: 25c periods 10-24, 25d periods 2-5, 25g periods 20-30.
###############################################################################

### 25a. Create a disruption scenario. Expect 201, Location naming it,
# numReplications 100, seed echoed, eventCount 0 and an
# empty events array.
# @name createScenario
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/scenarios
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Tier-1 plant fire",
  "numReplications": 200,
  "seed": 424242
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@scenarioId = {{createScenario.response.body.$.id}}


### 25b. List the project's scenarios. Expect 200 with one row carrying
# eventCount 0 and NO events array — the sidebar wants a row per
# scenario, not every bar of every timeline.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/scenarios
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25c. Add event 1 of 3 — the plant burns. A NODE target, authored against the
# baseline network. Expect 201 with the timing echoed back as 10 DAY and 2 WEEK,
# never as periods: on this network that is periods 10 to 24 of 52, but the
# stored event says what the researcher typed.
# severity 0.75 leaves a quarter of the plant's capacity; LINEAR ramps it back
# across the window; probability 0.4 means it occurs in 40% of replications, so
# the scenario is a distribution of futures rather than one.
# @name createNodeEvent
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{plantId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 10, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 2, "unit": "WEEK" },
  "severity": 0.75,
  "recoveryProfile": "LINEAR",
  "probability": 0.4
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@nodeEventId = {{createNodeEvent.response.body.$.id}}


### 25d. Add event 2 of 3 — the SUP-1 → PLANT-1 truck leg is cut, stated in
# hours. Expect 201. 36 HOUR is periods 2 (36/24 = 1.5, NEAREST) and 3 DAY is 3,
# so the bar runs 2 to 5 — mixed units in one scenario, which is exactly why the
# events are ordered by the derived second count and not by the stated value.
# recoveryProfile and probability are omitted: STEP and 1.0, a certain event
# whose capacity snaps back at the end of the window.
# @name createLinkEvent
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "LINK",
  "targetId": {{link1Id}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 36, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "duration": { "value": 3, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 1.0
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@linkEventId = {{createLinkEvent.response.body.$.id}}


### 25e. The region catalogue of this network. Expect 200 and two
# rows — EU-North with 2 nodes (DC-1, CUST-1) and EU-West with 2 (SUP-1,
# PLANT-1), alphabetically. Derived from node.region rather than stored, so
# retagging the last node of a region removes it from this list.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/regions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25f. Resolve one tag to its node set — the preview the scenario builder
# shows before a REGION event is saved. Expect 200 with nodeCount 2 and the two
# EU-West nodes in id order. This runs the resolution itself, so what the
# builder previews and what a run will expand the event into cannot differ.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/region-nodes?region=EU-West
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25g. Add event 3 of 3 — a regional power failure. No targetId at all: a
# REGION event is addressed by targetRegion, and carrying both halves is
# refused (25p). Expect 201, targetId absent from the response, periods 20 to 30
# on this clock.
# @name createRegionEvent
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "REGION",
  "targetRegion": "EU-West",
  "startOffset": { "value": 20, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 10, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5,
  "recoveryProfile": "EXPONENTIAL",
  "probability": 0.15
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@regionEventId = {{createRegionEvent.response.body.$.id}}


### 25h. Read the scenario back. Expect 200, eventCount 3, and the events in
# TIMELINE ORDER — the link event (36 h = 129,600 s) first, then the node event
# (10 d), then the region event (20 d). Ordered by the derived second count,
# which is the only ordering that is correct once one scenario mixes hours,
# days and weeks.
GET {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25i. The same bars, without the scenario around them — for a client that
# already has the scenario. Expect the same three, same order.
GET {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25j. Drag the plant-fire bar: a full replacement, which is what the timeline
# sends because it holds the whole event. Moved to 20 DAY, shortened
# to 5 DAY, severity raised to 0.9, and made certain. Expect 200 — 20 + 5 = 25,
# still inside the 52-period horizon.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/events/{{nodeEventId}}?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{plantId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 20, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 5, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.9,
  "recoveryProfile": "LINEAR",
  "probability": 1.0
}


### 25k. Duplicate the scenario — a deep copy, events included. The
# body is optional and so is Content-Type: this is the one write in the API that
# declares no `consumes`. Expect 201, a new id, eventCount 3, and three events
# with new ids and identical timings and units.
# @name duplicateScenario
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/duplicate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Tier-1 plant fire — delayed onset"
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@copyScenarioId = {{duplicateScenario.response.body.$.id}}


### 25l. Duplicate again with no body at all. Expect 201 and the derived name
# "Tier-1 plant fire (copy)". Send this one with no Content-Type header.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/duplicate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25m. Rename the original and drop its fixed seed. Expect 200, seed absent
# from the response (null members are dropped) — meaning a fresh seed is drawn
# per run, and the one used is recorded on the run either way.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Tier-1 plant fire (revised)",
  "numReplications": 500
}


### 25n. Delete one bar. Expect 204; 25o then shows two events left.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/events/{{regionEventId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25o. Confirm: eventCount 2, and the copy made in 25k still has 3 — a
# duplicate is a copy, not a view.
GET {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25p. Delete the first copy. Expect 204; its events cascade.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{copyScenarioId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# SCENARIO FAILURES — the event semantics, each refused with a `code`
#
# Four of these are the reason the endpoint takes a networkId at all: nothing
# about a target or a window can be judged without one.
###############################################################################

### 25q. severity 1.5. Expect 400, code VALIDATION_FAILED, fieldErrors naming
# `severity` — it is a capacity-availability multiplier *reduction* in [0,1],
# and 1.5 does not mean "150% offline", it means nothing.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{dcId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 1.5
}


### 25r. probability -0.2. Expect 400, code VALIDATION_FAILED, fieldErrors
# naming `probability`. Same rule, other field.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{dcId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5,
  "probability": -0.2
}


### 25s. An unknown recovery profile. Expect 400 — the enum is exactly
# {STEP, LINEAR, EXPONENTIAL}, each constant selecting a strategy bean
# that returns availability(t), so "GRADUAL" names no behaviour. This
# is Jackson refusing the value before bean validation runs, so the body is a
# problem+json without fieldErrors.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{dcId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5,
  "recoveryProfile": "GRADUAL"
}


### 25t. An unknown target. Expect 422, code EVENT_TARGET_INVALID: node 999999
# is not in this network. There is no foreign key that could have caught it —
# target_id points at node.id or link.id depending on target_type, and no
# constraint can express a reference whose table is chosen by another column.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": 999999,
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5
}


### 25u. A target from another network — the variant created in request 26.
# Expect 422, EVENT_TARGET_INVALID. RUN THIS AFTER REQUEST 26/27: fill
# @variantNodeId from request 27's response first. An id from a sibling variant
# is the plausible mistake here, not a made-up number, and it is the reason an
# event names the network it is authored against.
#
# @variantNodeId = <an id from request 27>
#
# POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
# Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
# Content-Type: application/json
#
# {
#   "targetType": "NODE",
#   "targetId": {{variantNodeId}},
#   "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
#   "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
#   "severity": 0.5
# }


### 25v. A region no node carries. Expect 422, EVENT_TARGET_INVALID naming the
# tag. Refused rather than warned about: the event would be stored, the run
# would complete, and the results would show a network shrugging off a
# disruption it never received — a false negative in the one direction a
# resilience study cannot afford.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "REGION",
  "targetRegion": "APAC",
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5
}


### 25w. Both halves of the reference at once. Expect 422,
# EVENT_TARGET_INVALID: a NODE event is addressed by targetId, and a row
# carrying a region as well would have two answers to what it strikes — the
# timeline groups by one and the engine resolves the other. ck_event_target
# forbids it in the schema too; this is the readable version of that refusal.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{dcId}},
  "targetRegion": "EU-North",
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5
}


### 25x. A REGION event with no region. Expect 422, EVENT_TARGET_INVALID.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "REGION",
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5
}


### 25y. An event that outruns the horizon, as a refusal. Expect 422, code
# EVENT_EXCEEDS_HORIZON, and a body carrying startPeriod 30, windowPeriods 28,
# endPeriod 58 and horizonPeriods 52 — because "extend the horizon" is only
# actionable next to how far the event overruns it.
#
# The offset and the window are discretised SEPARATELY, as the engine will
# discretise them: 30 DAY -> period 30, 4 WEEK -> 28 periods, 58 > 52.
# Rounding their sum instead would report an end period no simulation uses.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{dcId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 30, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 4, "unit": "WEEK" },
  "severity": 0.5
}


### 25z. A duration with no unit. Expect 400, VALIDATION_FAILED, fieldErrors
# naming `duration.unit`. The same rule as request 32 — a value without a unit
# is the bug the pair exists to make impossible.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{dcId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 3 },
  "severity": 0.5
}


### 25aa. A zero-length event. Expect 400, code BAD_REQUEST. Zero is a
# legitimate duration nearly everywhere else — a same-period lead time, no dwell
# at a DC — so DurationDto stays @PositiveOrZero and this check lives in the
# service. An event that lasts no time has no recovery window, so its recovery
# profile parameterises nothing (ck_event_window).
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{scenarioId}}/events?networkId={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{dcId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 0, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5
}


### 25ab. A scenario name already in use in the project. Expect 409,
# DUPLICATE_NAME (uq_scenario).
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/scenarios
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Tier-1 plant fire (revised)"
}


###############################################################################
# EVENT_EXCEEDS_HORIZON AS A *FINDING* — the other half of the check
#
# 25y proves the API refuses to store an event that outruns the horizon, which
# raises the obvious question: how can the editor's warning banner ever report
# one? Two ways, and both matter. The event may have been written against a
# variant with a longer horizon — scenarios are project-scoped — or
# the network's time base may have changed afterwards. The second is what these
# three requests demonstrate.
#
# The arithmetic is shared: TimeValidationInput.EventWindow is the single
# implementation, so the banner and the 422 cannot come to disagree about which
# period an event ends in.
###############################################################################

### 25ac. Shrink the horizon to 12 periods. Expect 200. Nothing about the
# scenario is checked here — PUT /time-base validates the network's own
# durations, and it names no scenario.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "horizonPeriods": 12,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 25ad. Now ask the pair question. Expect 200, errorCount 1, warningCount 5 —
# the four findings of request 20 plus two the scenario contributes:
#
#   DISRUPTION_EVENT  NODE <plant>  window      EVENT_EXCEEDS_HORIZON    ERROR
#   DISRUPTION_EVENT  LINK <link1>  startOffset DURATION_ROUNDING_ERROR  WARNING
#
# The first is the plant-fire event as 25j left it: period 20, lasting 5, ending
# at 25 — after a horizon of 12. The error sorts to the top.
#
# The second is a reminder that an event's own durations are checked like any
# other: the link event starts at 36 HOUR, which is 1.5 days and rounds to 2 on
# this clock (+33%). The link event's window, 2 + 3 = 5, still fits.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-validation?scenarioId={{scenarioId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 25ae. Put the clock back to the 52 periods the worked example uses, so the
# rest of the file runs against the documented network.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "horizonPeriods": 52,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


###############################################################################
# VARIANTS
###############################################################################

### 26. Clone the network as a configuration variant (FR-09).
# Expect 201: the whole structure copied — 4 nodes, 3 links, both node_product
# rows — under a new network with version 1 of its own name, baseline false.
# The copy inherits the clock: same period, horizon and rounding policy, since
# two networks compared side by side must discretise identically.
# @name cloneNetwork
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/clone
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Baseline + backup supplier",
  "leverChanges": {
    "sourcing": { "addedBackupSupplier": "SUP-2" },
    "redundancy": { "capacityMultiplier": 1.2 }
  }
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@variantNetworkId = {{cloneNetwork.response.body.$.network.id}}


### 27. Confirm the copy is a full one — expect the four nodes again, with new
# ids and their units intact: PLANT-1 still 400 per WEEK, not 57.14 per DAY.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{variantNetworkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 28. The provenance tree of the baseline — expect one variant with its lever
# diff.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/variants
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# DELIBERATE FAILURES — each must be refused, with problem+json and a `code`
###############################################################################

### 29. Self-loop. Expect 422, code LINK_SELF_LOOP.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceNodeId": {{plantId}},
  "targetNodeId": {{plantId}}
}


### 30. Duplicate link — the same ordered pair as request 9.
# Expect 409, code LINK_DUPLICATE, carrying existingLinkId.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceNodeId": {{supplierId}},
  "targetNodeId": {{plantId}}
}


### 31. Cross-network reference — a link in the *variant* network whose source is
# a node of the *base* network. Expect 422, code LINK_CROSS_NETWORK.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{variantNetworkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceNodeId": {{supplierId}},
  "targetNodeId": {{plantId}}
}


### 32. A duration with no unit. Expect 400, code VALIDATION_FAILED, with
# fieldErrors {"leadTime.unit": "unit is required"} — the whole point of FR-13
# is that a bare number is never guessed at.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceNodeId": {{plantId}},
  "targetNodeId": {{customerId}},
  "leadTime": { "value": 3 }
}


### 33. A rate spelled like a duration. Expect 400 — "unit" is not a field of a
# rate, and fail-on-unknown-properties refuses it rather than dropping it and
# defaulting the real timeUnit. Compare request 5.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "SUP-2",
  "type": "SUPPLIER",
  "capacity": { "value": 500, "unit": "DAY" }
}


### 34. Negative values inside the unit objects. Expect 400, code
# VALIDATION_FAILED, naming capacity.value and processingTime.value — the
# nested path, not a single opaque message for the request.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "SUP-3",
  "type": "SUPPLIER",
  "capacity": { "value": -500, "timeUnit": "DAY" },
  "processingTime": { "value": -2, "unit": "HOUR" }
}


### 35. A period of zero. Expect 400, code BAD_REQUEST: every conversion
# divides by the period, and ck_network_period says the same thing in
# the schema.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 0, "unit": "DAY" },
  "horizonPeriods": 52,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 35b. A period under one second. Expect 400, code BAD_REQUEST. The stated
# value is positive, so it passes bean validation, but the schema makes the
# canonical form a whole number of seconds and 0.4 s rounds to 0 — which
# ck_network_period rejects. Caught in the service so the caller gets a message
# naming the rule rather than a constraint violation from the driver.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 0.4, "unit": "SECOND" },
  "horizonPeriods": 52,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 36. A horizon of zero. Expect 400, code VALIDATION_FAILED, fieldErrors
# {"horizonPeriods": "horizonPeriods must be at least 1"}.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "DAY" },
  "horizonPeriods": 0,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}


### 37. Changing the time base of a frozen network — see request 68c.
#
# This network has no simulation run against it, so the request below would
# succeed and prove nothing. The refusal it was written for now has a home:
# request 68c submits a run, which freezes its network from the moment the run
# row is written, and then attempts exactly this against it. Expect
# 409 and code NETWORK_IMMUTABLE there, carrying networkId — results are stated
# in periods, so redefining the period turns "TTR = 3" from three days into
# three hours without changing a stored number. The remedy is
# request 26: clone, and set the time base on the variant.
#
# PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/time-base
# Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
# Content-Type: application/json
#
# {
#   "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "HOUR" },
#   "horizonPeriods": 1248,
#   "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
# }


### 38. Unauthenticated — no Authorization header at all.
# Expect 401, code UNAUTHENTICATED, WWW-Authenticate: Bearer.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects


### 39. Bad token. Expect 401, code UNAUTHENTICATED.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects
Authorization: Bearer not.a.real.token


### 40. Bean validation — blank name, probability out of [0,1], bad latitude.
# Expect 400, code VALIDATION_FAILED, with a fieldErrors member.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "   ",
  "type": "SUPPLIER",
  "failureProb": 5,
  "lat": 300
}


### 41. Duplicate node name within a network. Expect 409, code DUPLICATE_NAME.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "SUP-1",
  "type": "SUPPLIER"
}


### 42. Wrong password. Expect 401, code INVALID_CREDENTIALS, and no hint as to
# which half was wrong.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{username}}",
  "password": "definitely-not-the-password"
}


###############################################################################
# DATA IMPORT AND EXPORT (FR-02, FR-14)
#
# Requests 44-56. The sample datasets and what each should report are documented
# in samples/README.md; file paths below are relative to this file.
#
# NOTE ON MULTIPART: the "< ./path" line inserts the file's content. Each part
# needs its own Content-Disposition and a blank line before the content. The
# boundary string (SNRM) is arbitrary but must match the Content-Type header and
# the closing --SNRM-- exactly.
#
# Validation failure comes back as 200 with the report, not as problem+json:
# the report IS the answer, and RFC 7807 has nowhere to put a hundred rows. Only
# a malformed request (no files, unknown project) is a 4xx.
###############################################################################

### 44. Preview the 6-node sample — step 1→2 of the wizard. Expect 200 with five
# sheets, their headers, row counts, a sample of rows, and the canonical schema.
# `timeBase.declared` is true because network_meta.csv is attached.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/import/preview
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=SNRM

--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="network_meta.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/network_meta.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="nodes.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/nodes.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="links.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/links.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="products.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/products.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="node_products.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/node_products.csv
--SNRM--


### 45. Dry run of the same upload — step 3 of the wizard. Expect 200 with
# valid=true, committed=false, errorCount=0, warningCount=0, and NO network
# created. Note that nodes.csv and links.csv carry no *_unit column at all: the
# values are read in the period unit from network_meta.
