@simNetworkId = {{importSimulationSample.response.body.$.networkId}}

### 61. The imported network's nodes, so the event below can name PLANT-1's id.
# @name simNodes
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{simNetworkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

# nodes.csv order is SUP-1, PLANT-1, CUST-1, and the importer assigns ids in
# file order — so PLANT-1 is the second row.
@plantNodeId = {{simNodes.response.body.$[1].id}}

### 62. The scenario of docs/simulation-verification.md §1.4. Expect 201.
# seed and numReplications are set here; request 65 overrides the replication
# count to 1 so the run is hand-checkable.
# @name simScenario
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/scenarios
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Plant outage",
  "numReplications": 100,
  "seed": 20260802
}

@simScenarioId = {{simScenario.response.body.$.id}}

### 63. The one event: PLANT-1 at half capacity for three days from day 3.
# Expect 201. STEP recovery, probability 1.0 — nothing about this run is random.
POST {{baseUrl}}/scenarios/{{simScenarioId}}/events?networkId={{simNetworkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "targetType": "NODE",
  "targetId": {{plantNodeId}},
  "startOffset": { "value": 3, "unit": "DAY" },
  "duration": { "value": 3, "unit": "DAY" },
  "severity": 0.5,
  "recoveryProfile": "STEP",
  "probability": 1.0
}

### 64. Submit with an event that names nothing in this network. Expect 422,
# code EVENT_TARGET_UNRESOLVED, with eventIds listing the offender — the
# cross-variant case DisruptionScenarioService cannot catch at write time.
# Run this only after re-targeting an event by hand; it is here to document the
# refusal, not as part of the happy path.
#
# POST {{baseUrl}}/simulations
# Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
# Content-Type: application/json
#
# { "networkId": {{metricNetworkId}}, "scenarioId": {{simScenarioId}} }

### 65. Submit the run of docs/simulation-verification.md §1.5. Expect 202 with
# a jobId, a runId, status QUEUED and the fully resolved params — including the
# seed, which is what makes the run replayable.
#
# One replication and safetyStockPriority 0, so the table in §4.3 is derivable
# by inspection: replenishment is off and nothing is random. The run still
# executes TWO replications — the undisrupted baseline set is not
# optional, and TTR, LOSS_AREA, DISRUPTION_COST_DELTA and RESILIENCE_INDEX are
# undefined without it. Expect `replications: 2` in the response.
# @name submitSimulation
POST {{baseUrl}}/simulations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "networkId": {{simNetworkId}},
  "scenarioId": {{simScenarioId}},
  "params": {
    "replications": 1,
    "seed": 20260802,
    "demandNoiseCv": 0,
    "timingJitterPeriods": 0,
    "safetyStockPriority": 0
  }
}

@jobId = {{submitSimulation.response.body.$.jobId}}
@runId = {{submitSimulation.response.body.$.runId}}

### 66. Poll the job. Expect 200 and, on a network this small,
# status DONE with progress 1.0 on the first poll. `resourceId` is the runId.
GET {{baseUrl}}/jobs/{{jobId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 67. The metric suite. Expect 200 and exactly these eleven values — every one
# of them derived by hand in docs/simulation-verification.md §8:
#
#   FILL_RATE              0.94                 (470 / 500)
#   SERVICE_LEVEL          0.7                  (7 of 10 periods served in full)
#   MIN_FILL_RATE          0.8
#   TTR                    3.0                  displayUnit DAY
#   LOSS_AREA              0.5999999999999999   (3 x 0.2, in fill-rate.periods)
#   TOTAL_COST             6315.0
#   DISRUPTION_COST_DELTA  390.0                (6315 - 5925)
#   CVAR_COST              6315.0
#   RESILIENCE_INDEX       0.9142857142857143   (32 / 35)
#   AVG_INVENTORY          5.0                  (50 / 10 — see §6.6)
#   AVG_PIPELINE           0.0                  (nothing is ever in flight here)
#
# Every row is NETWORK-scoped with runId set. Every ciLow and ciHigh is null:
# one replication has no sample variance, and CVAR_COST carries no interval at
# any replication count. Only TTR has a displayUnit — it is the one
# time-valued metric in the suite.
#
# AVG_PIPELINE is a row reading 0.0, NOT an absent row. A zero pipeline is a
# measurement this network's zero lead times produce; the absent case is the
# four disruption-relative metrics on a baseline run (request 81).
# samples/four-echelon-playback/ §6.1 is where the pair is non-trivial:
# AVG_INVENTORY 22.0 and AVG_PIPELINE 29.0.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}/results
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 68. The per-period curves. Expect 200 and the ten points of §7 — both the
# disrupted curve and the undisrupted baseline one, which is what makes the
# resilience triangle drawable without recomputation.
#
#   period 0    served 50   cost 375   baselineServed 50   baselineCost 375
#   period 1    served 50   cost 350   baselineServed 50   baselineCost 350
#   period 2    served 50   cost 650   baselineServed 50   baselineCost 650
#   periods 3-5 served 40   cost 780   baselineServed 50   baselineCost 650
#   periods 6-9 served 50   cost 650   baselineServed 50   baselineCost 650
#
# Sum of `cost` = 6315 and of `baselineCost` = 5925, matching §4.3 and §4.4.
#
# Each point also carries the two columns V9__element_timeseries.sql added:
# `endingInventory` is 50 in period 0 and 0 in every other period (PLANT-1's
# stock, and no other node here ever holds anything), and `inPipeline` is 0 in
# ALL TEN — both lead times are zero and no node dwells, so nothing is ever in
# flight. That zero is the point of the network, not a gap; see §7.1 and
# samples/four-echelon-playback/ for the case where it is not zero.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}/timeseries
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 68b. The whole run in one call. Expect 200 with the run record,
# the same eleven metrics and the same ten points. `run.params` is what
# actually ran, read back from params_json.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 68c. The network is now frozen. Expect 409, code NETWORK_IMMUTABLE, with
# networkId — a run in QUEUED, RUNNING or DONE holds its network, and
# results are stated in periods, so redefining the period would turn "TTR = 3
# days" into "TTR = 3 hours" without changing a stored number.
# The remedy is to clone (request 26) and set the time base on the variant.
#
# This is the case request 37 could not provoke until the simulation engine
# existed.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{simNetworkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "horizonPeriods": 240,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}

### 68d. Cancel a finished job. Expect 200, not an error — cancellation is
# idempotent, so the UI's stop button does not have to win a race.
# The status comes back DONE, unchanged.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/jobs/{{jobId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 68e. Poll a job that does not exist. Expect 404 — which does not mean work
# was lost: jobs are held in memory and bounded by snrm.jobs.retention, while
# the run and its results are durable in simulation_run.
GET {{baseUrl}}/jobs/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 68f. Submit against a network with no demand. Expect 422, code
# NETWORK_HAS_NO_DEMAND — every simulated metric would report perfect service
# for every scenario, which is indistinguishable from a resilient network.
# Uses the metric-verification network's project-mate only if it has customers;
# run this against a network you have stripped of demand.
#
# POST {{baseUrl}}/simulations
# Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
# Content-Type: application/json
#
# { "networkId": {{networkId}}, "scenarioId": {{simScenarioId}} }

### 68g. Submit against a run of 100 replications — the default, and
# the point at which the confidence intervals mean something.
# Expect 202, then a job that takes visibly longer. `replications: 200` in the
# response, because the baseline set doubles it.
#
# The suite's values will differ from §8 only in that ciLow and ciHigh are now
# populated on every metric except CVAR_COST; with no noise and no jitter the
# 100 replications are identical, so each interval collapses to a point.
# @name submitFullRun
POST {{baseUrl}}/simulations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "networkId": {{simNetworkId}},
  "scenarioId": {{simScenarioId}}
}


###############################################################################
# COMPARISON AND EXPORT — requests 69–74
#
# These read what requests 60–68 produced. Run at least one simulation first,
# or every simulated cell comes back null with a NO_RUN note — which is itself
# worth seeing once.
###############################################################################

### 69. The cross-variant metric matrix. Expect 200.
#
# What to check, in order:
#   * `variants` is in the order networkIds names them — a researcher building
#     a table to read left to right gets the columns they asked for.
#   * every `rows[].cells[]` is aligned by index with `variants`, and a null is
#     "not measured", never zero.
#   * `rows[].direction` is the calculator's own declaration, and the
#     structural rows — DENSITY, AVG_PATH, CLUSTERING — are NEUTRAL with no
#     cell marked `best`. That is a decision, not a gap: none of the three is
#     an objective.
#   * exactly one code carries `timeValued: true` — TTR — and its `unit` is the
#     finest period unit among the compared variants. Each of its
#     cells carries both the converted `value` and the raw `periods`.
#   * NODE_CRITICALITY is absent. It is one row per node and belongs
#     to the dashboard's table, not to a matrix whose rows are metrics.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison?networkIds={{simNetworkId}}&networkIds={{variantNetworkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 70. The same, pinned to one scenario. Expect 200 and no MIXED_SCENARIOS
# note. Without ?scenarioId= each column takes its network's most recent DONE
# run whatever it applied — so one column can be a plant outage and the next a
# port closure, and the lever change gets the credit for the difference.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison?networkIds={{simNetworkId}}&networkIds={{variantNetworkId}}&scenarioId={{simScenarioId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 71. Every network in the project — omit networkIds. Expect 200, and
# expect notes: any network that has never been simulated produces a NO_RUN
# note and fills only the topological half of its column. This is the request
# that shows MIXED_TIME_BASES if request 68c's period change was committed,
# since that variant then steps in hours while the others step in days.
# The values stay comparable — TTR is converted — but the models
# are not identical, and the note says so.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 72. A network from another project. Expect 404, with the same message a
# missing id gives: telling the caller an id exists elsewhere is information
# they have not earned (NetworkLookup makes the same choice).
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison?networkIds=999999
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 73. Export the run — three tables as one workbook.
#
# `run` is provenance, params_json verbatim, seed included. `metrics` writes a
# time-valued metric twice: `value` in periods and `value_in_display_unit`
# beside its unit, so TTR reads as 14 and as 14 days. `timeseries` carries both
# curves plus `fill_rate_loss`, the height of the resilience triangle at each
# period.
#
# NOTE on that column: summing it gives a *lower bound* on LOSS_AREA, not the
# metric. These rows are replication averages while the metric takes
# each replication's shortfall before averaging — the same quantity with the
# mean and the max(0, …) in the other order. They coincide exactly on a
# deterministic run, which is what §8 of docs/simulation-verification.md uses.
# `cost_delta` is linear and does sum exactly to DISRUPTION_COST_DELTA.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}/export?format=xlsx
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 73b. The same as a zip of three CSV files — the form to diff or read into
# R. `?format=csv` and `?format=zip` are synonyms, and both are case-insensitive.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}/export?format=CSV
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 73c. An unrecognised format. Expect 400 naming the two that work. There is
# no `xml` here on purpose: the XML interchange document exists for a
# *network*, and a metric table has no way back in — offering it would produce
# a document nothing can read.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}/export?format=xml
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 74. Export the comparison matrix. Expect 200 and a workbook of three
# tables: `comparison`, `variants` and `notes`.
#
# Each variant occupies three columns — value, ci_low, ci_high — so every cell
# stays a number a reader can sort and chart. `best` names the winning variant
# or the tied ones, and is empty on a NEUTRAL row. `variants` carries
# lever_changes, which is what makes an exported matrix a finding rather than a
# scoreboard. `notes` travels with the file deliberately: a
# matrix separated from its "these use different period lengths" warning is a
# matrix that will be misread.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison/export?networkIds={{simNetworkId}}&networkIds={{variantNetworkId}}&format=xlsx
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# PROJECT ARCHIVE
#
# The XML archives a network's *inputs*. This archives the whole
# experiment: the project, every network, every scenario and event, and every
# completed run with its parameter set, metric results and time series — which
# is what makes an entire experiment archivable alongside the thesis, and what
# guards against single-case validation.
#
# Run these after the simulation block, so the project has completed runs to
# archive. Save the response of 75 to disk, then upload that file in 76.
###############################################################################

### 75. Archive the project. Expect 200, `application/zip`, and a
# Content-Disposition naming it `<project>-archive.zip`.
#
# Inside: `bundle.json` with the manifest, the catalogue, the scenarios, the
# runs and their results; and `networks/001-<name>@v1.xml`, one XML
# interchange document per network — byte-identical to what request 55 returns,
# so a single network can be lifted out of the zip and imported on its own.
#
# The manifest records the simulation engine version. A stored parameter set
# replays exactly only against the engine that wrote it, so the
# restore compares the two and says so.
#
# REST Client writes the body to a file with the "Save Response Body" button;
# curl is easier if you plan to re-upload it:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -OJ \
#     http://localhost:8080/api/v1/projects/1/archive
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/archive
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 76. Restore it into a NEW project. Expect 201, a Location header naming the
# new project, and an ArchiveReport.
#
# Read three things in the response. `engineMatches` — false means the archived
# results came from a different engine and must not be placed beside a locally
# computed run without re-running. `restoredCounts` against `sourceCounts` —
# any shortfall is explained by a finding. And `findings` itself, which is
# empty on a clean restore.
#
# Never a merge: the archive always lands in a new project, and the name is
# suffixed "(restored)" if this user already has one by that name. Every run it
# creates carries `importedAt` and `sourceRunId` — check with request 77.
#
# Point `< ./` at wherever you saved request 75's response.
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/archive/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----SnrmArchiveBoundary

------SnrmArchiveBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="archive.zip"
Content-Type: application/zip

< ./archive.zip
------SnrmArchiveBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Resilience study (replication)
------SnrmArchiveBoundary--

### 77. A restored run reports its provenance. Expect `importedAt` non-null and
# `sourceRunId` naming the id it held where it was computed.
#
# This is the point of the two columns V7 adds. A restored run is a genuine
# DONE run — it freezes its network and it appears in the
# comparison view, both correct — so these fields are the only thing
# distinguishing a number this installation computed from one it was handed.
# Replace {{restoredRunId}} with a run id from the restored project.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{restoredRunId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 78. Not an archive. Expect 422 with code ARCHIVE_UNREADABLE.
#
# A format version this build does not know is refused the same way, rather
# than parsed optimistically — the rule XmlSchema applies to network XML. The
# *engine* version is the opposite case and only warns: a reader that cannot
# parse a document cannot warn about it either, but one that parses a document
# from an older engine can say exactly what it found.
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/archive/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----SnrmArchiveBoundary

------SnrmArchiveBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="not-an-archive.zip"
Content-Type: application/zip

this is not a zip
------SnrmArchiveBoundary--


###############################################################################
# CLEANUP (optional) — removes the project and everything under it.
###############################################################################

### 59. Delete the project. Expect 204. Every network under it cascades,
# imported ones included, and each network's metric_result rows with them.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# FR-17 - THE BASELINE RUN, PROVISIONAL FIGURES, AND COMPARISON BY RUN
###############################################################################

### 79. A baseline run: no scenarioId at all. Expect 202, and `replications`
# equal to N rather than 2N - an undisrupted run has no disruption to isolate,
# so the paired set is skipped rather than computed twice.
# Replications and seed come from params here, since there is no scenario to
# read defaults from.
POST {{baseUrl}}/simulations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "networkId": {{networkId}},
  "params": { "replications": 1, "seed": 20260803 }
}

### 80. Poll it. While RUNNING, expect a `partial` object - the provisional
# figures of FR-17: {replicationsDone, replicationsTotal, fillRate,
# minFillRate, totalCost}. Streaming statistics over the completed
# replications only: no confidence intervals, nothing that needs the whole
# set (CVAR_COST above all). Once the status is terminal, `partial` is null -
# the persisted suite supersedes it, which is why a client must label these
# provisional and never persist or export them.
# Replace {{baselineJobId}} with request 79's jobId. A one-replication run
# finishes fast; use 100+ replications to actually watch the figures move.
GET {{baseUrl}}/jobs/{{baselineJobId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 81. The baseline run's suite. Expect FILL_RATE, SERVICE_LEVEL,
# MIN_FILL_RATE, TOTAL_COST, CVAR_COST, AVG_INVENTORY and AVG_PIPELINE - and
# NO TTR, LOSS_AREA, DISRUPTION_COST_DELTA or RESILIENCE_INDEX. Those four are
# defined against a disruption and nothing here was disrupted: an absent row is
# unmeasured, never zero, and every reader must already treat it that way.
# The inventory pair is on the OTHER side of that line - it
# describes what the network held, which a run with no scenario answers as
# well as any other, so both rows are present.
# The run record itself carries scenarioId: null, which is how a client knows
# to label it "Baseline".
# On samples/four-echelon-playback/ this is the seven-row table of that
# sample's README §6.1, with AVG_INVENTORY 22.0 and AVG_PIPELINE 29.0.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{baselineRunId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 82. Compare by RUN, not by network (FR-17). Expect one column per named
# run, in request order - which is what expresses the two questions the
# network-keyed form cannot: this network's baseline beside its disruption
# run, and one network under two scenarios. Expect the MIXED_SCENARIOS note
# to fire, correctly: baseline-versus-disruption measures ONLY the scenario,
# and the note is the row of text that says so.
# Replace the ids with request 79's runId and any DONE scenario run of the
# same network.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison?runIds={{baselineRunId}},{{scenarioRunId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 83. The two selectors are mutually exclusive. Expect 400 BAD_REQUEST -
# columns are keyed either by network or by run, and a request carrying both
# is ambiguous about which it asked.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison?runIds={{baselineRunId}}&networkIds={{networkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 84. A run that is not DONE cannot be a column. Expect 409 RUN_NOT_DONE:
# unlike a network with no run (which keeps a column and shows its
# topological half), a half-finished run has nothing to show YET, and an
# empty column that silently fills in on the next request would be a matrix
# that changes meaning between two renders of one URL.
# Submit a slow run first and use its runId here before it finishes.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison?runIds={{runningRunId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 85. The run-keyed matrix, exported. Same selector, same file shape; two
# runs of one network get disambiguated column headers - "Baseline v1 -
# Plant outage (run 13)" - because two columns named identically would make
# the spreadsheet's `best` cell ambiguous.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/comparison/export?runIds={{baselineRunId}},{{scenarioRunId}}&format=xlsx
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# FR-18 - THE PER-ELEMENT TIME SERIES
#
# What every node and every link did in every period, which GET /timeseries
# aggregates away and cannot be disaggregated back into. These three read the
# 4-echelon baseline run of samples/four-echelon-playback/ - re-import that
# network FRESH (the Stage-1 copy is frozen by a pre-V9 run) and submit with no
# scenarioId, then set @elementRunId to the runId it returned.
#
# Every expected value below is derived by hand in that sample's README:
# on-hand in §6.5.1, flows in §6.5.2, arrivals in §6.5.3, inbound leads in
# §6.5.4, and the baseline copy-in in §6.5.5. Node and link ids are whatever
# the importer assigned - use request 61's shape (GET /networks/{id}/nodes) to
# find them; the ORDER is SUP-1, PLANT-1, DC-1, CUST-1 and then a, b, c.
###############################################################################

@elementRunId = 14
@dcNodeId = 3
@linkCId = 3

### 86. Every element's series. Expect 200, `available: true`, four nodes and
# three links, and every array 30 long (the network's horizon).
#
#   nodes, `onHand`  SUP-1   10, 10, 20, 10, then 10 to t=29
#                    PLANT-1  5, 15, 15,  5, then  5
#                    DC-1    15, 15,  5,  5, then  5
#                    CUST-1  10,  0,  0,  0, then  0
#   nodes, `served`  CUST-1 is 10 in all thirty periods; every other node 0,
#                    and every `unserved` is 0 everywhere (§6.5.1)
#   nodes, `throughput`
#                    SUP-1 is 10 in every period EXCEPT t=3, where it is 0;
#                    PLANT-1, DC-1 and CUST-1 are 0 in all thirty. Every leg
#                    has a lead time, so inbound material lands in the hold and
#                    never crosses the target's capacity arc (§6.5.1).
#   nodes, `arrivals`
#                    PLANT-1  0, 10, 10,  0, then 10
#                    DC-1     0, 10,  0, 10, then 10
#                    CUST-1   0,  0, 10, 10, then 10
#                    SUP-1 is 0 throughout - nothing is inbound to an origin.
#   nodes, `inboundLead`
#                    SUP-1   null in ALL thirty periods (no inbound arc)
#                    PLANT-1 1.0, 1.0, null, 1.0, then 1.0
#                    DC-1    1.0, null, 1.0, 1.0, then 1.0
#                    CUST-1  null, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, then 1.0
#                    A null is a claim - nothing was dispatched toward it that
#                    period. A 0.0 would say it arrived instantly (§6.5.4).
#   links, `flow`    a S>P  10, 10,  0, 10, then 10
#                    b P>D  10,  0, 10, 10, then 10
#                    c D>C   0, 10, 10, 10, then 10
#   links, `utilisation`
#                    flow / 100 - so 0.1 wherever the flow is 10 and exactly
#                    0.0 (NOT null) wherever it is 0: an idle arc at full
#                    availability is idle, not unmeasurable (§6.5.2).
#   `availability`   1.0 on every node and every link in every period.
#   baseline columns This is a baseline run, so `baselineOnHand`,
#                    `baselineServed` and `baselineFlow` are COPIES of
#                    `onHand`, `served` and `flow`, value for value - the
#                    copy-in convention of V9__element_timeseries.sql. Assert
#                    equality, not absence (§6.5.5).
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{elementRunId}}/timeseries/elements
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 87. One node. Expect 200 with the SAME envelope - `available: true`,
# `nodes` holding exactly one entry, `links` empty. Point it at DC-1 and expect
# `onHand` 15, 15, 5, 5, then 5 to t=29, `inboundLead` 1.0, null, 1.0, 1.0,
# then 1.0, `served` 0 throughout (demand sits on CUSTOMER nodes) and
# `throughput` 0 throughout.
#
# A node id this run recorded nothing for answers `available: true` with an
# EMPTY list - the run recorded, that element did not appear. That is a
# different statement from `available: false`, which means the run holds no
# series at all: still QUEUED or RUNNING, older than V9, or submitted with
# params.recordElementTimeseries = false. Neither is an error.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{elementRunId}}/timeseries/nodes/{{dcNodeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 88. One link. Same envelope, `links` holding one entry and `nodes` empty.
# Point it at arc c (DC-1 > CUST-1) and expect `flow` 0, 10, 10, 10, then 10,
# `utilisation` 0.0, 0.1, 0.1, 0.1, then 0.1, `availability` 1.0 throughout and
# `baselineFlow` equal to `flow`.
#
# Read `flow` as what was DISPATCHED. Arc c carries 0 at t=0 because CUST-1
# opened at its order-up-to target of 20 and ordered nothing, and the 10 it
# dispatches at t=1 arrives at CUST-1 at t=2 - which is why request 86's
# `arrivals` for CUST-1 is 0, 0, 10, 10. That one-period offset is the whole
# visual argument of this sample (§6.5.3, §8.3).
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{elementRunId}}/timeseries/links/{{linkCId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# FR-20 - DISCARDING A RUN, AND THE FREEZE IT RELEASES
#
# The exit from immutability. A run that is KEPT is exactly as network-freezing
# as it ever was; what FR-20 adds is the ability to say the result was never
# worth keeping. Because the freeze is DERIVED from the run rows
# rather than stored on the network, deleting the last locking run makes the
# network editable again with nothing else to reset - no flag, no second call.
#
# Run 65-68c first: they leave a DONE run ({{runId}}) against a frozen network
# ({{simNetworkId}}), which is exactly the state this block operates on.
###############################################################################

### 89. What is actually holding this network frozen. Expect 200 and one entry
# per run, NEWEST FIRST, as records without their results.
#
# This is the read the discard confirmation is built from: it has to
# state how many results are about to be destroyed, and warn separately when
# one of them is a RESTORED ARCHIVE RESULT - `importedAt` non-null, meaning
# those numbers were computed by another installation and this may be the only
# copy here. Check the run of request 65 is listed with status DONE,
# `importedAt: null` and `sourceRunId: null`.
#
# An empty list is an ordinary answer: a network nobody has run yet. Compare
# with `editable` on GET /networks/{id} - this list is the whole and only
# reason that flag is false.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{simNetworkId}}/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 90. Refused while a job owns the run. Expect 409 and code RUN_ACTIVE, with
# a detail naming DELETE /jobs/{jobId} as what to do first.
#
# QUEUED and RUNNING are the two states in which a worker either holds the run
# or is about to. Cancellation is cooperative and never interrupts,
# so deleting the row underneath a worker would leave it writing a status, a
# metric suite and three time series against an id that no longer exists. Two
# calls, and the caller chooses between them.
#
# To provoke it: submit a run with 500+ replications against
# samples/four-echelon-playback/, paste its runId below, and send this while
# the poll still reports RUNNING. Then cancel its job and send it again -
# CANCELLED deletes cleanly.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{activeRunId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 91. Discard the DONE run of request 65. Expect 204 and no body.
#
# Everything the run produced goes with it, by declared ON DELETE CASCADE:
# metric_result (fk_mr_run, V2), run_timeseries (fk_timeseries_run, V2),
# run_node_timeseries and run_link_timeseries (fk_node_ts_run / fk_link_ts_run,
# V9). Nothing is deleted in application code - one definition of what a run
# owns, and it is the schema's.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 92. The cascade left nothing behind. Expect 404 with code NOT_FOUND - the
# run row is gone, so every read keyed on it is gone with it.
#
# Send 67 and 68 again for the same answer: the metric suite and the curves
# have no run to belong to. The per-element series of request 86 likewise.
GET {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{runId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 93. THE POINT OF THE WHOLE FEATURE. Expect `editable: true`.
#
# Request 68c got 409 NETWORK_IMMUTABLE against this same network. Nothing has
# been reset, unlocked or notified - the last locking run simply stopped
# existing, and NetworkMutationGuard derives the freeze from the run rows on
# every mutation. Send 68c again now and it succeeds.
#
# The network's TOPOLOGICAL metric rows survive: they carry run_id = NULL and
# belong to the network rather than to any run, and nothing about the structure
# changed. GET /networks/{id}/metrics/topological still answers.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{simNetworkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 94. Refused WHOLE while any run is active. Expect 409 RUN_ACTIVE, a detail
# that names the runs in the way, and - the part worth checking - NOTHING
# DELETED. Send request 89 again afterwards and the completed runs are all
# still listed.
#
# Active runs are deliberately not skipped. A half-discard would destroy every
# completed result and leave the network frozen anyway, since an active run
# locks it too - the one thing the caller asked for would be the one
# thing they did not get.
#
# To provoke it: submit one long run against {{networkId}} and send this while
# it executes.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 95. Discard every run of a network in one request. Expect 204.
#
# The editor's "discard this network's runs and edit in place". One
# request rather than N, because the question is about the NETWORK - stop this
# being frozen - and a client looping over runs would release the freeze
# somewhere inside its own loop with nothing to say if the third call failed.
#
# Send it twice: the second time is also 204. A network with no runs is what
# the caller asked for, and reporting a failure on the second click of one
# button would be a lie about the state.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 96. Another user's run. Expect 404 with code NOT_FOUND - the same answer
# every other run read gives, and for the same reason: confirming that an id
# exists elsewhere is information the caller has not earned. With
# one research user in Phase 1 this fires only for an id that does not exist,
# which is the same 404.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/simulations/999999
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 97. A restored archive run deletes like any other. Expect 204.
#
# Request 77 leaves a run with `importedAt` set in the restored project. Paste
# its runId below. Refusing here would make a restored project permanently
# uneditable, which is the opposite of what the archive is for - so the warning
# that these numbers were computed elsewhere and this may be the only copy
# belongs in the confirmation dialog, not in a refusal.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/simulations/{{restoredRunId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# EXPORTING A SUBSET AS A STANDALONE PROJECT (FR-24)
#
# `GET /projects/{id}/archive` gains an optional `?networkIds=`. The archive it
# writes is the ordinary archive narrowed to those networks, so
# restoring it through the same POST yields a separate project holding exactly
# them. It COPIES and never moves.
#
# Three rules decide what a subset carries, and each is the safe answer rather
# than the tidy one:
#
#   1. the WHOLE product catalogue travels — a node_product row names a product
#      by name, so a computed subset risks a reference nothing resolves, and an
#      unused entry is harmless where a dangling one is not;
#   2. EVERY scenario travels, and an event whose target sits in a network you
#      did not select restores dangling (a negative target_id) so the scenario
#      visibly refuses to run rather than running with a disruption missing;
#   3. a variant edge whose base network is not selected is DROPPED, and the
#      manifest counts it rather than passing over it.
#
# By this point the project holds several networks — {{networkId}} (built by
# hand), {{variantNetworkId}} (its clone), {{importedNetworkId}},
# {{xmlNetworkId}}, {{metricNetworkId}} and {{simNetworkId}}. Requests 98-99 are
# the round trip; 100 is the copy assertion; 101-102 are the refusals; 103 is
# the promise that the no-parameter form did not move.
###############################################################################

### 98. Archive THREE of the project's networks. Expect 200, `application/zip`,
# and a Content-Disposition naming it `<project>-3-networks-archive.zip` — the
# count is in the filename because the subset is a copy and is meant to sit
# beside the whole-project archive of request 75 rather than overwrite it in a
# downloads folder.
#
# Inside, `bundle.json` carries a `manifest.selection` the whole-project archive
# has no member for at all:
#
#   "selection": {
#     "selectedNetworks": 3, "projectNetworks": 6,
#     "networkKeys": [ … ], "droppedVariantEdges": N, "excludedEventTargets": N
#   }
#
# `droppedVariantEdges` counts fork notes whose base network did not travel —
# {{variantNetworkId}} was cloned from {{networkId}}, so selecting the clone
# without its base is exactly that case. `excludedEventTargets` counts events
# pointing into a network you left behind.
#
# Save the response to disk for request 99:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -OJ \
#     "http://localhost:8080/api/v1/projects/1/archive?networkIds=1&networkIds=2&networkIds=3"
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/archive?networkIds={{simNetworkId}}&networkIds={{variantNetworkId}}&networkIds={{metricNetworkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 99. Restore the subset. Expect 201 and an ArchiveReport whose
# `restoredCounts.networks` is 3 — exactly the networks named in 98, and no
# others.
#
# Read the findings. `ARCHIVE_IS_SUBSET` is new and sits directly under any
# engine warning, because it qualifies what is *present* where the engine one
# qualifies every number. It states that the archive is a copy, that the whole
# catalogue travelled (so a product no restored network uses is expected rather
# than a fault), that every scenario travelled, and — when either happened —
# how many event targets and how many fork notes did not.
#
# `restoredCounts` will NOT show a shortfall: the manifest counted what the
# subset holds, so the arithmetic agrees. The finding is the only sign, which is
# the whole reason it exists.
#
# Point `< ./` at wherever you saved request 98's response.
POST {{baseUrl}}/projects/archive/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----SnrmArchiveBoundary

------SnrmArchiveBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="subset.zip"
Content-Type: application/zip

< ./subset-archive.zip
------SnrmArchiveBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Three networks (subset)
------SnrmArchiveBoundary--

### 100. THE COPY ASSERTION. Expect every network of the source project still
# here, {{simNetworkId}}, {{variantNetworkId}} and {{metricNetworkId}} included,
# each still Frozen or Editable exactly as it was.
#
# FR-24 says the export "copies and never moves: the selected networks, their
# runs and their results stay in the project that computed them". Nothing in
# `ProjectArchiveService` writes anything — the whole class is
# @Transactional(readOnly = true) — and this is that stated as a check rather
# than as an annotation. Confirm the runs too with GET /networks/{id}/runs.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/networks
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 101. A SCENARIO WHOSE EVENT TARGETS AN EXCLUDED NETWORK. Expect 422 with
# code EVENT_TARGET_UNRESOLVED.
#
# This is carry rule 2 doing its job. The scenario travelled — it is
# project-scoped and replayable across variants — but its event pointed at a
# node of a network you did not select, so it restored with a NEGATIVE
# target_id: a sentinel no AUTO_INCREMENT id can collide with, the same
# convention an already-dangling target restores through.
#
# The event is therefore visible, structurally intact, and unrunnable. Dropping
# it instead would have left a scenario that runs happily with one disruption
# missing — a network shown absorbing something it never received, which is the
# one direction a resilience study cannot afford to be wrong in.
#
# Replace both ids with a network and a scenario from the project request 99
# created. Repoint the event in the scenario builder and it runs.
POST {{baseUrl}}/simulations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "networkId": {{restoredSubsetNetworkId}},
  "scenarioId": {{restoredSubsetScenarioId}}
}

### 102. A network id from ANOTHER project. Expect 404, code NOT_FOUND, and a
# detail naming the offending id and saying it was refused rather than skipped.
#
# Never a silently smaller archive. Skipping the id would write a file the
# caller would restore later into a project quietly missing a configuration —
# discovered, if at all, as an absent column in a comparison view. "Not yours"
# and "does not exist" are the same answer, as everywhere else.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/archive?networkIds={{simNetworkId}}&networkIds=999999
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 103. `networkIds` present but naming nothing. Expect 400 BAD_REQUEST telling
# you to omit the parameter instead.
#
# An empty selection and an omitted one mean opposite things — "none of them"
# and "all of them" — so reading the first as the second would hand back the
# whole project to a caller who asked for a subset of it.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/archive?networkIds=
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 104. THE NO-PARAMETER ARCHIVE IS UNCHANGED. Expect 200 and a
# Content-Disposition naming it `<project>-archive.zip` — the old name, not the
# subset one — and a `bundle.json` whose `manifest` has NO `selection` member at
# all.
#
# This is request 75 again, and that is the point: the whole-project archive is
# the reproducibility artefact and it does not get to change shape
# because a second caller appeared. The selection travels as a manifest member
# that is null on this path, and `spring.jackson.default-property-inclusion` is
# `non_null`, so it is not written. Diff this against the file request 75
# produced: only `exportedAt` differs.
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/{{projectId}}/archive
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


###############################################################################
# IMPORTING A NETWORK AS A VARIANT OF ANOTHER (FR-28)
#
# A project arrives as a folder of workbooks: one network per file, the wizard
# asks which file is the baseline and what each of the others is, and a file
# that is a CONFIGURATION VARIANT of the baseline is imported with the optional
# `baseNetworkId` form field. That field is the ONLY backend addition the whole
# feature needs.
#
# The edge is written in the SAME TRANSACTION that creates the network, one
# statement after it. Two things follow, and both are the point:
#
#   * an imported variant is never briefly a network with no edge, so the
#     provenance tree cannot draw a batch mid-import as a forest of roots;
#   * a rolled-back import leaves no orphan edge either - there is no
#     intermediate state to reconcile because there is no intermediate state.
#
# NOTHING IS DIFFED. This tool does not compare two networks, so `leverChanges`
# is the note the request carries or nothing at all. Structural diffing of an
# imported variant against its base is named as future work in
# ImportService.recordVariantEdge; an invented diff would read as a lever the
# researcher chose rather than an artefact of two files.
#
# A BATCH IS N OF THESE REQUESTS, sequenced by the wizard, baseline first -
# there is no batch endpoint, because the per-file transaction and the per-file
# report are exactly what one call already is.
#
# Requests 44-54 must have run: 105 forks {{importedNetworkId}}, the
# minimal-6-node import of request 44.
###############################################################################

### 105. Import a workbook as a VARIANT of an existing network. Expect 201 and
# the ordinary import report - plus, invisibly in this response, a
# CONFIGURATION_VARIANT row pointing at {{importedNetworkId}}. Request 106 is
# where you see it.
#
# Note what is NOT sent: `baseline`. The project's baseline flag and the base of
# this batch's variant edges are two different things, and the wizard's roles
# step keeps the names apart because conflating them is how the wrong thing gets
# marked. Sending both is legal and means both.
#
# `leverChanges` is free-form JSON and is the only thing that ever fills the
# variant's note. Omit it and the edge is an ordinary edge with a null note.
# @name importVariant
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=SNRM

--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="projectId"

{{projectId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Dual sourcing (imported variant)
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="baseNetworkId"

{{importedNetworkId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="leverChanges"

{"note": "second supplier qualified in EU-South"}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="network_meta.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/network_meta.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="nodes.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/nodes.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="links.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/links.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="products.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/products.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="node_products.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/node_products.csv
--SNRM--

@variantImportId = {{importVariant.response.body.$.networkId}}

### 106. THE EDGE, THROUGH THE SURFACE THAT DRAWS THE TREE. Expect 200 and an
# entry whose `network.id` is {{variantImportId}}, `baseNetworkId` is
# {{importedNetworkId}}, `generatedBy` is MANUAL and `leverChanges` is the JSON
# object of request 105 - as real JSON, not a string containing JSON.
#
# MANUAL because a person chose these files and said what each one was; SEARCH
# is reserved for candidates the Phase 2 configuration engine persists.
# This is the same list an editor fork (request 26) appears in, and
# that is the whole reason the edge is recorded rather than the imported network
# being left in the table with no parent anyone could name.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{importedNetworkId}}/variants
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 107. THE SAME REQUEST AS A DRY RUN. Expect 200, `committed: false`,
# `networkId: null` - and no new edge.
#
# `validateOnly` creates no network, so it creates no edge either: an edge whose
# network was never created is a foreign key with nothing behind it, not a
# lesser version of the feature. Send request 106 again afterwards and the list
# is exactly as long as it was.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=SNRM

--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="projectId"

{{projectId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Never created
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="baseNetworkId"

{{importedNetworkId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="validateOnly"

true
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="nodes.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/nodes.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="links.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/links.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="node_products.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/node_products.csv
--SNRM--

### 108. A BASE FROM ANOTHER PROJECT. Expect 422, code
# BASE_NETWORK_OUT_OF_SCOPE, and a detail naming both projects. NOTHING IS
# CREATED - not the network, and certainly not a network with the edge quietly
# dropped, which would be indistinguishable from a file the wizard was told was
# independent.
#
# It is a 4xx rather than a row in the validation report because the report
# answers for the user's DATA - the cells of their file, which is why it travels
# as a 200 - while `baseNetworkId` is a request field the wizard composed, in
# the same class as `projectId`. LINK_CROSS_NETWORK and PRODUCT_OUT_OF_SCOPE are
# the precedents, and both are 422: the ids are real, their combination is not
# something one project can express.
#
# Replace the id below with a network from the project request 76 or 99
# restored into - GET /projects/{thatId}/networks lists them.
@foreignNetworkId = 999998
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=SNRM

--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="projectId"

{{projectId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Orphan
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="baseNetworkId"

{{foreignNetworkId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="nodes.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/nodes.csv
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="links.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/links.csv
--SNRM--

### 109. A BASE THAT DOES NOT EXIST. Expect 404, code NOT_FOUND - the same
# answer every other id gives, and the same answer another user's network would
# get. Missing and not-yours are one answer everywhere in this API.
#
# Note that it fires on a DRY RUN too, which is deliberate: the base is resolved
# before the file is staged, because the wizard sends one base id with every
# file of a batch and a wrong one should be caught before the first of eight
# commits rather than after the seventh.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=SNRM

--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="projectId"

{{projectId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Orphan
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="baseNetworkId"

999999
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="validateOnly"

true
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="nodes.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/nodes.csv
--SNRM--

### 110. `leverChanges` that is not JSON. Expect 400, code BAD_REQUEST, naming
# the field.
#
# The column is a MySQL JSON column and the comparison view renders these notes
# as real JSON, so accepting text here would move the failure to a
# reader that can do nothing about it. Same treatment as `mapping`, and for the
# same reason: a JSON string in a flat form field, because a browser's FormData
# appends strings.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=SNRM

--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="projectId"

{{projectId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Bad note
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="baseNetworkId"

{{importedNetworkId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="leverChanges"

second supplier qualified
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="nodes.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

< ./samples/minimal-6-node/nodes.csv
--SNRM--


###############################################################################
# RENAMING A NETWORK THAT HAS BEEN RUN (FR-29)
#
# The freeze covers what a result was computed FROM: nodes, links, per-product
# rows, the time base. What it has always meant is that a network's NAME and
# its BASELINE FLAG are none of those. Neither is an input to any metric and
# neither is read by any calculator, so freezing them protected nothing the rule
# exists to protect, while making a network unnameable for the rest of its life
# on the strength of one trial run.
#
# It is not a corner case. A batch import names each network after its file
# (FR-28), so `Baseline_v3_FINAL.xlsx` becomes a row in a table that will
# outlive the file - and deciding which configuration is the project's baseline
# is a judgement a researcher usually reaches AFTER comparing runs.
#
# Requests 111-112 freeze a network so that 113-116 have something to work on.
# If you have run 65-68c and NOT yet run 91, {{simNetworkId}} is already frozen
# and 113-116 can point at it instead.
###############################################################################

### 111. Freeze a network: one baseline run, one replication, against the
# imported minimal network. Expect 202 with a jobId and a runId.
# @name renameRun
POST {{baseUrl}}/simulations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "networkId": {{importedNetworkId}},
  "params": { "replications": 1, "seed": 20260808 }
}

@renameJobId = {{renameRun.response.body.$.jobId}}

### 112. Poll until DONE, then confirm the freeze with the read below. Expect
# `editable: false` - a run in QUEUED, RUNNING or DONE holds its network,
# and this flag is derived from those rows and from nothing else.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{importedNetworkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 113. THE POINT OF FR-29. RENAME IT. Expect 200, the new name, and
# `editable: false` - still frozen, because the rename did not release anything.
# It was never covered.
#
# Send request 89's read against this network afterwards: the run that freezes
# it is still there, still DONE, still pointing at the same nodes and links. The
# input of that result did not change, which is the entire question the freeze
# asks.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{importedNetworkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Dual sourcing, EU-South",
  "baseline": false
}

### 114. THE TIME BASE OF THE SAME FROZEN NETWORK. Expect 409, code
# NETWORK_IMMUTABLE, with networkId. This is the request that makes FR-29 a
# carve-out rather than a hole.
#
# The guard came off ONE method - NetworkService.update - and off nothing else.
# The time base is its most consequential use: results are stated in periods, so
# redefining a period turns this run's "TTR = 3" from three days into three
# hours without changing a stored number. The results are not wrong, they are no
# longer comparable, and nothing about them looks different. The
# remedy is unchanged: clone (request 26), and set the clock on the variant.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{importedNetworkId}}/time-base
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "periodLength": { "value": 1, "unit": "HOUR" },
  "horizonPeriods": 240,
  "roundingPolicy": "NEAREST"
}

### 115. A NEW NODE ON THE SAME FROZEN NETWORK. Expect 409, code
# NETWORK_IMMUTABLE. NodeService, LinkService, ProductService's node-product
# writes, the bulk canvas endpoints and DELETE /networks/{id} are all exactly as
# guarded as they were - structure is structure.
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{importedNetworkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "PLANT-2",
  "type": "PLANT",
  "capacity": { "value": 400, "timeUnit": "DAY" }
}

### 116. THE TRAP WORTH KNOWING: name and baseline flag travel together. Expect
# 200 - and read `baseline` in the response.
#
# PUT replaces both fields, so a rename that omits `baseline` CLEARS it. That is
# why the UI edits the two as one control on the project table rather than
# offering a rename that quietly drops a flag; it is the same "name and unit
# value together" rule the product catalogue already states.
#
# Two further things to try here, both now possible and neither previously:
#   * mark this frozen network as the project's baseline (`"baseline": true`,
#     below) - accepted, unless another network already holds the flag, which is
#     a 409 BASELINE_ALREADY_SET naming the incumbent;
#   * clear the flag on a FROZEN incumbent and set it on another network. Two
#     requests, because the check refuses a second baseline and never moves the
#     flag for you. While update was guarded this was a dead end: the incumbent
#     could not be cleared, so BASELINE_ALREADY_SET could never be resolved and
#     the project's baseline stayed on whichever network happened to be run
#     first.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{importedNetworkId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Dual sourcing, EU-South",
  "baseline": true
}


###############################################################################
# CAPTIONS ON NODES AND LINKS (FR-30) — requests 117-127
#
# A node or a link carries a short `caption` and a `captionVisible` flag. It is
# an attribute of the element, not a view preference: it persists, it travels in
# all three interchange formats beside pos_x/pos_y, and it is refused on a
# frozen network exactly like any other edit.
#
# THREE RULES WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU SEND ANYTHING
#
#   1. AN EMPTY CAPTION DRAWS NOTHING, whatever the flag says. So the flag is a
#      control over annotation that exists, not a way to reserve space.
#   2. AN OMITTED captionVisible MEANS VISIBLE. Not false. That holds on a PUT,
#      in a spreadsheet column and in an XML attribute, and it is why V10's
#      column is NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE - typing a caption shows one, without a
#      second gesture, and every pre-existing row is already correct under it.
#   3. THE BULK PATCH CAN CLEAR A CAPTION, by sending an EMPTY STRING, and it is
#      the only field on which it can clear anything. A caption is an ordinary
#      edit written through the editor's debounced PATCH, and a PATCH cannot
#      express "set to nothing"; giving `""` a meaning on this one field is
#      how the two are reconciled. Request 120 is that.
#
# Requests 117-124 need the happy-path network of requests 1-14 ({{networkId}},
# {{dcId}}, {{link1Id}}). 125-126c are the round trip and need {{projectId}}.
# 127 needs the network requests 111-112 froze. Like the FR-28 and FR-29 blocks
# above, this one assumes request 59 - the project teardown - has NOT been sent.
###############################################################################

### 117. Create a node WITH a caption and no flag. Expect 201, the caption back,
# and `captionVisible: true` - rule 2. Nothing had to be ticked.
# @name captionedNode
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "DC-9",
  "type": "DC",
  "capacity": { "value": 350, "timeUnit": "DAY" },
  "posX": 520,
  "posY": 420,
  "caption": "Nordic hub - 3PL operated, 2 shifts"
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@captionedNodeId = {{captionedNode.response.body.$.id}}


### 118. Caption an existing node through the BULK PATCH - the request the
# editor's property panel actually sends when it flushes. Expect 200,
# and note that nothing else about {{dcId}} moved: omitted means unchanged, as
# for every other field on this DTO.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "nodes": [
    { "nodeId": {{dcId}}, "caption": "Single-sourced; 6-week qualification" }
  ]
}


### 119. HIDE a caption without losing it. Expect 200, `captionVisible: false`
# and the SAME caption text still in the response.
#
# This is what FR-30 exists for - "annotation can be written once and shown or
# hidden without being lost" - and it is why the flag is a column rather than
# the caption simply being deleted for a screenshot. The checkbox bulk-applies
# across a multi-selection where the text does not: hiding every
# caption at once is what preparing a figure wants, while writing one sentence
# onto five different nodes is not.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "nodes": [
    { "nodeId": {{dcId}}, "captionVisible": false },
    { "nodeId": {{captionedNodeId}}, "captionVisible": false }
  ]
}


### 120. THE CLEARING PATH. Expect 200 and `caption: null` on {{dcId}}.
#
# An EMPTY STRING is a write here; null would have been "leave it alone". Send
# `"caption": null` instead and compare the two responses - the first clears,
# the second does nothing, and that difference is the whole of rule 3.
#
# Read `captionVisible` in the response too: it is still false from request 119.
# Clearing the text does not touch the flag, and it does not need to - an empty
# caption draws nothing either way.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "nodes": [
    { "nodeId": {{dcId}}, "caption": "" }
  ]
}


### 121. A caption on a LINK, on exactly the same terms. Expect 200. A link's
# caption is drawn under the label it already carries - its declared lead time -
# because Cytoscape gives one label per element at one font size.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "links": [
    { "linkId": {{link1Id}}, "caption": "Contracted road leg, no alternative carrier" }
  ]
}


### 122. 201 characters. Expect 400 with a violation naming `caption` and the
# message "caption must be at most 200 characters". 200 is the column width of
# V10, and it is part of the feature rather than a storage limit:
# the caption has to sit UNDER the element's label in smaller type without
# competing with the name, and an element is identified by its name everywhere
# else in the tool (uq_node, the archive's ElementRef, FR-25's by-name matching).
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "nodes": [
    { "nodeId": {{dcId}}, "caption": "ccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc" }
  ]
}


### 123. THE PUT CLEARS BY OMISSION, like every other nullable field. Expect 200
# with `caption: null` - and `captionVisible: true`, NOT false.
#
# That asymmetry is deliberate and is rule 2 again: a PUT replaces, so an
# omitted caption is cleared, but an omitted FLAG is the documented default
# rather than the primitive one. A PUT that dropped the flag to false would make
# the next caption typed into this node invisible.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/nodes/{{captionedNodeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "DC-9",
  "type": "DC",
  "capacity": { "value": 350, "timeUnit": "DAY" },
  "posX": 520,
  "posY": 420
}


### 124. Read the network's nodes back. Expect {{dcId}} with `caption: null` and
# `captionVisible: false`, and DC-9 with `caption: null` and
# `captionVisible: true`. Both have no caption; they differ only in a flag
# nothing can currently observe, which is exactly why any default is safe on a
# row that has never been captioned - and why V10 needed no data migration.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{networkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 125. THE ROUND TRIP, PART 1 - import the XML sample, which now carries
# captions three ways: DC-1 shown, PLANT-1 written-and-hidden, and SUP-1 plus
# the PLANT-1 -> DC-1 link with a caption and NO captionVisible attribute at
# all. Expect 201, 0 errors, 0 warnings.
# @name importCaptionedXml
POST {{baseUrl}}/networks/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=SNRM

--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="projectId"

{{projectId}}
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="name"

Captioned XML
--SNRM
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="network.xml"
Content-Type: application/xml

< ./samples/xml-6-node/network.xml
--SNRM--


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@captionedXmlNetworkId = {{importCaptionedXml.response.body.$.networkId}}


### 126. Confirm the omission rule survived the import. Expect SUP-1 with its
# caption and `captionVisible: TRUE` - the attribute was absent in the file and
# an absent flag is visible - and PLANT-1 with its caption and
# `captionVisible: false`, which the file did state.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{captionedXmlNetworkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 126b. THE ROUND TRIP, PART 2 - export it again as XML. Expect 200,
# application/xml, and every caption back. Diff it against
# samples/xml-6-node/network.xml: the one difference is that SUP-1 and the
# PLANT-1 -> DC-1 link now carry an explicit `captionVisible="true"` where the
# sample omitted it. Same meaning, no longer resting on a default - the export
# writes both halves of the pair, exactly as it writes a unit beside an
# unconstrained capacity.
#
# THIS IS ALSO THE ARCHIVE. `GET /projects/{id}/archive` stores each network as
# the bytes of this call (archive/package-info.java), so a captioned network
# survives an archive and a restore because of this response and nothing else.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{captionedXmlNetworkId}}/export?format=xml
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 126c. And as a workbook. Expect 200 and an .xlsx whose `nodes` sheet ends
# with `caption` and `caption_visible`, and whose `links` sheet does too. Open
# it, edit a caption in Excel, and re-import it through request 44's form: the
# round trip now carries annotation as well as layout.
GET {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{captionedXmlNetworkId}}/export?format=xlsx
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 127. A CAPTION ON A FROZEN NETWORK. Expect 409, code NETWORK_IMMUTABLE.
#
# This is the request that draws the boundary of the freeze. A caption is
# read by no engine, exactly like the network name FR-29 exempted from the
# guard, and the exemption was deliberately NOT extended to it. Three reasons,
# and the third decides it: a caption sits on an element INSIDE the network, so
# exempting it means the editor's one debounced save queue has to know which
# fields in a batch are exempt and split the request - the guard stops being one
# call at the top of a service and becomes per-field logic in two places. The
# network's name cost nothing, because it is one field on one endpoint that
# writes nothing else. And the remedy here is already one click: fork the
# network (request 26), and annotate the variant.
#
# Needs the network requests 111-112 froze. The node id below is a placeholder
# and does not need to be real: `guard.assertMutable` is the FIRST line of
# NodeService.patchAll, before any id is resolved, so the refusal happens before
# the batch is looked at. That ordering is itself worth seeing - the guard is
# one call at the top of the method, which is precisely the shape an exemption
# would have destroyed.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/networks/{{importedNetworkId}}/nodes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "nodes": [
    { "nodeId": 1, "caption": "Annotated for the paper" }
  ]
}
